---
import ScvriaLayout from "../layouts/ScvriaLayout.astro";
---

<ScvriaLayout
  title="Manifesto — SCVRIA Sofware"
  description="The design direction and operating principles behind SCVRIA Sofware."
>
  <article class="scvria-page scvria-prose-page">
    <header class="scvria-page-header">
      <p class="scvria-eyebrow">Principles</p>
      <h1>Manifesto</h1>
      <p class="scvria-lead">Bring the intelligence back to the people.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="scvria-principles">
      <section>
        <h2>Inspectable systems</h2>
        <p>Prefer open-source software and systems people can inspect, understand, modify, and learn from.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>User agency</h2>
        <p>Design for meaningful control over tools, workflows, configuration, and the decisions software makes on a user's behalf.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Community over lock-in</h2>
        <p>Favor participation, interoperability, and replaceable parts over dependencies that make leaving unnecessarily difficult.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Local when it fits</h2>
        <p>Use local-first or offline-capable approaches when they improve resilience, privacy, ownership, or practical independence.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Open formats, owned data</h2>
        <p>Where applicable, prefer durable open formats and make it practical for users to keep, move, and understand their own data.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Useful before sprawling</h2>
        <p>Prefer small tools that solve real problems over unnecessary platform complexity. Guerrilla software can be disciplined, legible, and useful.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <p class="scvria-closing-claim">Bring the intelligence back to the people.</p>
  </article>
</ScvriaLayout>
